        <mxCell id="tls-vwc-arrow-l" connectable="0" parent="tls-vwc-arrow" style="edgeLabel;fontSize=7;fillColor=none;" value="reads caBundle" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <mxPoint x="-15" as="offset" />
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="tls-explain" parent="1" style="text;html=1;fontSize=9;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" value="&lt;b&gt;How it works:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;1. cert-manager creates the TLS secret (auto-renewed)&lt;br&gt;2. Volume projection mounts it as fullchain.pem / privkey.pem / cacerts.pem into the operator pod&lt;br&gt;3. The operator starts an HTTPS server on :8443 using those certs&lt;br&gt;4. The ValidatingWebhookConfiguration contains the &lt;b&gt;caBundle&lt;/b&gt; (CA cert, base64-encoded)&lt;br&gt;5. When API server receives a Kafka/KRaft/SR/etc. CR, it POSTs to the webhook over HTTPS&lt;br&gt;6. API server validates the webhook&#39;s TLS cert against the caBundle — if it matches, trust is established" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="100" width="620" x="880" y="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="tls-san-note" parent="1" style="text;html=1;fontSize=9;fontStyle=2;fontColor=#C62828;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" value="&lt;b&gt;SAN requirement:&lt;/b&gt; The cert must include &lt;code&gt;confluent-operator.&amp;lt;namespace&amp;gt;.svc&lt;/code&gt; as a DNS SAN,&#xa;because that&#39;s the service name the API server connects to." vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="25" width="580" x="880" y="240" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="adm-flow" parent="1" style="shape=mxgraph.gcp2.standard_rectangle;fillColor=#E3F2FD;strokeColor=#1565C0;fontStyle=1;fontSize=13;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=5;rounded=1;arcSize=8;" value="2. Admission Flow — What Happens When You Apply a CR" vertex="1">
